<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-diff-derivprops" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Properties of the derivative</title>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-diff-linear">
	<title>Linearity</title>
	
	
	<statement>
		<p>
				Consider the linear combination <m>f = \alpha g + \beta h</m> of functions <m>g</m> and <m>h</m>, where <m>\alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{R}</m>. The derivative of <m>f</m> satisfies <me>\frac{df}{dx} = \alpha \frac{dg}{dx} + \beta \frac{dh}{dx} .</me>
			</p>
	</statement>
	<proof>
		<p>
				This is a straightforward application of properties of the limiting process for functions. Suppose that <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> are both differentiable at a point <m>x=a</m>. <me>\begin{aligned}
\alpha g'(a) + \beta h'(a)
&amp;=\alpha \lim_{x \to a} \frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}
+\beta \lim_{x \to a} \frac{h(x)-h(a)}{x-a}, \text{ by defn.}\\
&amp;= \lim_{x \to a} \left ( \alpha \frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} + \beta \frac{h(x) - h(a)}{x-a} \right ), \text{ by algebra of limits}\\
&amp; = \lim_{x \to a} \left ( \frac{(\alpha g+ \beta h)(x) - (\alpha g + \beta h)(a)}{x-a}\right ), \text{ rearranging fractions}\\
&amp;= (\alpha g + \beta h)'(a), \text{ by definition}
\end{aligned}</me> The above equations demonstrate the linearity of the derivative.
			</p>
	</proof>
</theorem>

<theorem xml:id="thm-diff-product">
	<title>Product rule</title>
	<statement>
		<p>Consider the function <m>f=gh</m> which is a product of the two functions <m>g</m> and <m>h</m>. The derivative of <m>f</m> satisfies <me>\frac{df}{dx} = g \frac{dh}{dx} + h \frac{dg}{dx} .</me>
			</p>
	</statement>
	<proof>
		<p>Suppose that <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> are both differentiable at a point <m>x=a</m>. <me>\begin{aligned}
f'(a) &amp; = \lim_{x \to a} \frac{g(x)h(x) - g(a)h(a)}{x-a}, \text{def. of derivative and $f$}\\ \\
&amp;= \lim_{x \to a} \frac{g(x) (h(x) - h(a)) + h(a)(g(x)-g(a))}{x-a}, \text{preparatory manipulation}\\
&amp;=\left (\lim_{x \to a} g(x) \right )
\left ( \lim_{x \to a} \frac{h(x) - h(a)}{x-a} \right )
+ h(a) \left ( \lim_{x \to a} \frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x-a} \right ), \text{alg. of limits}\\
&amp;= g(a)h'(a) + h(a)g'(a), \text{known derivs.}\\
\end{aligned}</me> This is the required result.
</p>
	</proof>
</theorem>
<example>
	

<p><ol>
				<li>
							<p>
								Find the derivative of <m>y = (x^2 - 1) \sin{x}</m>. This is the product of the two functions <me>u(x) = x^2 -1 \text{  and  } v(x)= \sin{x},</me> whose derivatives are <me>\frac{du}{dx} = 2x \text{ and } \frac{dv}{dx} = \cos{x},</me> and so the required derivative is <me>\begin{aligned}
				\frac{dy}{dx} &amp;= u \frac{dv}{dx} + v \frac{du}{dx} \\
				&amp;= (x^2 - 1) \cos{x} + 2x \sin{x}.
				\end{aligned}</me>
							</p>
				</li>

				<li>
							<p>
								Find the derivative of <m>y=xe^x</m>....
							</p>
				</li>

			</ol></p>
</example>
			<paragraphs>
				<title>Linear approximations and associated errors</title>
				
				<p>
					Before moving on to consider other properties of differentiation we introduce another viewpoint on the derivative that will be needed for future proof arguments. Consider again the definition of the derivative of a function <m>f</m> at a point <m>a</m> in its domain. <me>f'(a) = \lim_{x \to a} \frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x-a}.</me> We reformulate this slightly by writing <m>x</m> as <m>x = a+k</m> and having the limiting process be as <m>k \to 0</m>, as follows <me>f'(a) = \lim_{k \to 0} \frac{f(a+k) - f(a)}{k}.</me> This suggests the existence of a function <m>\epsilon</m> that measures the discrepancy between <m>f'(a)</m> and the relative change quotient <m>(f(a+k) - f(a))/k</m>, defined as follows <me>\epsilon(k) = \frac{f(a+k) - f(a)}{k} - f'(a),</me> and this new function <m>\epsilon</m> has the important convergence property <me>\lim_{k \to 0} \epsilon(k) = 0.</me> We can now rearrange the definition of <m>\epsilon</m> to give the interesting expression <me>f(a+k) = f(a) + f'(a) k + k \, \epsilon(k).</me> This equation shows the <em>linear approximation</em>, <m>f(a) + k f'(a)</m>, to the value, <m>f(a+k)</m> of the function at a point close to <m>a</m>, with the associated <em>error term</em> <m>k \epsilon(k)</m>. The linear approximation is the linear function (i.e. line) that best approximates the function in the region around the point <m>x=a</m>. Not only does the error-term approach zero as <m>k \to 0</m> (as we would expect) but the error term divided by <m>k</m>, i.e. <m>\epsilon(k)</m>, converges to 0 as <m>k \to 0</m>.
				</p>
				<figure xml:id="diff-lin-approx">
				<caption>The linear approximation to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=a</m>.</caption>
			<image source="lin-approx.png" width="60%">
				<shortdescription></shortdescription>
				
			</image>
			</figure>
				<p>
					Picturing these various quantities with the aid of figure <xref ref="diff-lin-approx" /> may help. It’s important to appreciate how the linear approximation gives the equation of the tangent line to the graph of <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=a</m> in the form of <m>y(k) = f(a) + k f'(a)</m>, where <m>k = x-a</m>.
				</p>

				<p>
					This allows us to interpret the above to give an alternative definition of the derivative as follows:
				</p>

				<definition xml:id="def-deriv-lin-approx">
					<title>The derivative</title>
					
					
					<statement>
						<p>The derivative, <m>f'(a)</m>, is the unique number that provides the linear approximation <m>f(a) + f'(a) k</m> for the value <m>f(a+k)</m> with the property that the error term <m>k \epsilon(k)</m> has the convergence property that <m>\epsilon(k) \to 0</m> as <m>k \to 0</m>.
				</p>
					</statement>
				</definition>

				<p>
					This view of the derivative will allow us to give a rigorous proof of the chain rule. But it is also a useful to have such slightly different views of the same concept. This notion of linear approximations (and other higher-order approximations) and the behaviour of their associated error terms is important in the subject of <em>numerical analysis</em>.
				</p>
			</paragraphs>

			<theorem xml:id="thm-diff-chain">
				<title>Chain rule</title>
				
				
				<statement>
					<p>
					Consider the composition <m>y=f \circ g</m> of two functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>. Let <m>u=g(x)</m> so that <m>y=f(u)</m>. Then the derivative of <m>y</m> satisfies <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{dy}{du} \frac{du}{dx},</me> or in the alternative notation, at a point <m>x=a</m> we have <me>y'(a) = f'(g(a))g'(a).</me>
				</p>
				</statement>
				<proof>
					<p>
					<em>Proof.</em> We suppose that the function <m>g</m> is differentiable at the point <m>a</m> and that <m>f</m> is differentiable at the point <m>g(a)</m>. In the linear approximations view this means we have derivative quantities <m>g'(a)</m> and <m>f'(g(a))</m> and error functions <m>\epsilon</m> and <m>\mu</m> satisfying <me>\begin{aligned}
g(a+k) &amp;= g(a) + k g'(a) + k \epsilon(k) \\
f(g(a) + l) &amp;= f(g(a)) + l f'(g(a)) + l \mu(l),
\end{aligned}</me> with convergence properties <m>\epsilon(k) \to 0</m> as <m>k \to 0</m> and <m>\mu(l) \to 0</m> as <m>l \to 0</m>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Our task now is to prove the chain rule according to definition <xref ref="def-deriv-lin-approx" />, i.e. to examine the quantity <m>y(a+k)</m> and discover the linear approximation to it, showing that the derivative <m>y'(a)</m> has the form described by the chain rule and that the error term has the necessary convergence property. <me>\begin{aligned}
y(a+k) &amp;= f(g(a+k)), \text{ \small{defn. of composition}}\\
&amp;= f\big (g(a) + k g'(a) + k \epsilon(k) \big ), \text{ \small{from above for $g(a+k)$}}\\
&amp;= f(g(a)) + l f'(g(a)) + l \mu (l), \text{ \small{from above with $l=k g'(a) + k \epsilon(k)$}}\\
&amp;= f(g(a)) + k \big ( g'(a) f'(g(a)) \big ) + \lambda(k),
\end{aligned}</me> where the error term <m>\lambda(k)</m> is defined by <me>\lambda(k) = k \epsilon(k) f'(g(a)) + \big [ k g'(a) + k \epsilon(k) \big ] \mu (k g'(a) + k \epsilon(k)).</me> So we have established the required form for <m>y(a+k)</m> which shows the expected derivative <m>y'(a)</m> as asserted by the chain rule. To complete the proof we have just to prove that <m>\lambda(k) / k \to 0</m> as <m>k \to 0</m>. This can be seen by considering the equation <me>\frac{\lambda(k)}{k} = \epsilon(k) f'(g(a)) + \big [g'(a) + \epsilon(k) \big ] \mu (k g'(a) + k \epsilon(k)).</me> We can prove that <m>\lambda(k) / k \to 0</m> by applying the algebra of limits theorem to the above, noting that <m>k g'(a) + k \epsilon(k) \to 0</m> as <m>k \to 0</m>.
				</p>
				</proof>
			</theorem>

			<example>
				<p><ol>
					<li>
									<p>
										Differentiate <m>y=(x^2 -1)^5</m>.
									</p>
					
									<p>
										Let <m>u = x^2 -1</m> so that <m>y=u^5</m>. Then <m>\frac{du}{dx} = 2x</m> and <m>\frac{dy}{du} = 5u^4</m> and so <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{dy}{du} \frac{du}{dx} = 5u^4 2x = 5(x^2 - 1)^4 2x = 10x (x^2 -1)^4.</me>
									</p>
					</li>

					<li>
									<p>
										Differentiate <m>y= \sin{(2x^2 - x)}</m>.
									</p>
					
									<p>
										Let <m>u=2x^2 - x</m> so that <m>y=\sin{u}</m>. Then <m>\frac{du}{dx} = 4x - 1</m> and <m>\frac{dy}{du} = \cos{u}</m> and so <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{dy}{du} \frac{du}{dx} = \cos{(u)} (4x -1) = (4x-1) \cos{(2x^2 - x)}.</me>
									</p>
					</li>

				</ol></p>
			</example>
			<p>
					To complete the collection of basic properties of the derivative we need to know how to differentiate functions that are defined as quotients of other functions, i.e. how the derivative of the qutient depends on the derivatives of the numerator and denominator of the quotient. This is provided by the well-known quotient rule.
				</p>
	<theorem xml:id="thm-diff-quotient">
		<title>Quotient rule</title>
		<statement>
			<p>If <m>y</m> is the quotient of two functions <m>y=\frac{u}{v}</m> then <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{v \frac{du}{dx} - u \frac{dv}{dx}}{v^2}.</me> Or in the alternative notation <me>y'(a) = \frac{v(a)u'(a) - u(a)v'(a)}{v(a)^2}.</me>
				</p>
		</statement>
	</theorem>
	<p>
					The details of the proof of the quotient rule are left as an exercise. One way to prove it is to make use of the chain rule and product rule properties that we have already established together with the following lemma.
				</p>
	
	<lemma xml:id="lem-diff-recip">
		<title>Derivative of the reciprocal</title>
		<statement>
			<p>Consider the reciprocal function <m>r</m> defined on the non-zero reals, <m>\mathbb{R} \backslash \{0\}</m>, by <m>r(x) = 1/x</m>. The derivative of <m>r</m> at a point <m>a \neq 0</m> is given by <me>r'(a) = \frac{-1}{a^2}.</me>
				</p>
		</statement>

	</lemma>

	<example>
		<p>
				These examples illustrate the use of the quotient rule.
				</p>

				<p><ol>
					<li>
									<p>
										Differentiate <m>y = \frac{x^2 - 1}{\sin{x}}</m>.
									</p>
					
									<p>
										Let <m>u=x^2 -1</m> and <m>v= \sin{x}</m> then <m>\frac{du}{dx}= 2x</m> and <m>\frac{dv}{dx} = \cos{x}</m>. Then <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{v \frac{du}{dx} - u \frac{dv}{dx}}{v^2} = \frac{2x \sin{x} - (x^2 -1)\cos{x}}{\sin^2 x}.</me>
									</p>
					</li>

					<li>
									<p>
										Differentiate <m>y=\frac{2x-1}{3x^2 + x}</m>.
									</p>
					
									<p>
										Let <m>u=2x -1</m> and <m>v= 3x^2 +x</m> then <m>\frac{du}{dx}= 2</m> and <m>\frac{dv}{dx} = 6x+1</m>. Then <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{v \frac{du}{dx} - u \frac{dv}{dx}}{v^2} = \frac{2(3x^2 +x) - (2x-1)(6x+1)}{(3x^2+x)^2} = \frac{-6x^2 + 6x + 1}{(3x^2 + x)^2}.</me>
									</p>
					</li>

				</ol></p>
	</example>
			
</section>
